  "text": "Here's the implementation for the `remove_adjacent_duplicates` function, which processes a list of integers and removes all consecutive duplicates, leaving only a single instance of each group of duplicates:\n\n```python\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nimport unittest\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- The function starts by checking if the input list is empty. If it is, it returns an empty list.\n- It then initializes a result list with the first element of the input list.\n- It loops through the remaining elements of the list and appends an element to the result list **only if** it is **not equal** to the last element in the result list, ensuring consecutive duplicates are removed.\n\n### Example:\nFor input `[1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]`, the output will be `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.\n\nYou can run this script to run the unit tests and verify that the function works as expected.",
